{"version_id": 84, "article_id": 9, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Keeping global science collaboration alive during conflict needs fairer rules", "title_zh": "冲突时期想让全球科研合作不断，规则得改得更公平", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 209, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "Scientific collaboration has often been seen as a field that can rise above politics. But as global tensions grow, researchers warn that a scientist's institution is increasingly treated as a political label.", "lead_zh": "科研合作一向被认为能超越政治。但随着全球紧张局势加剧，研究人员警告说，科学家的所属机构正越来越被当成政治标签。", "preview": "Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasi", "preview_zh": "长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益影响国际研究，一个意想不到的结果正在出现：机构归属正在变成政治身份的替身。换句话说，研究人员所属的大学或研究所，如今可能被解读为其政治立场的标志。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 74, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e302484/content/audio/9/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 94.163, "size_bytes": 568800, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 236, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e302484/content/data/versions/84.json", "paragraphs": ["Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended result is appearing: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. In other words, the university or institute a researcher belongs to may now be read as a sign of their political side.", "This concern was raised in a correspondence published in Nature on 06 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University in China and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They argue that fairer rules are needed to maintain global science collaboration during conflict.", "The authors declare no competing interests. Their letter appears in volume 658 of Nature, page 572. The topic connects to wider debates, including academic boycotts, shifting research partnerships, and how geopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink collaboration. Several related articles in Nature also discuss ways to push back against politics stalling science.", "The message is simple but urgent: if affiliation is treated as political identity, researchers may be cut off from partners, data, and funding for reasons that have little to do with science. Fairer rules, the authors suggest, would help protect collaboration even when countries disagree."], "paragraphs_zh": ["长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益影响国际研究，一个意想不到的结果正在出现：机构归属正在变成政治身份的替身。换句话说，研究人员所属的大学或研究所，如今可能被解读为其政治立场的标志。", "这一担忧来自2026年10月6日发表在《自然》杂志上的一封通讯。作者是中国上海大学的Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh和位于金华的中国浙江师范大学的Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。他们认为，要在冲突期间维持全球科学合作，就需要更公平的规则。", "作者声明没有利益冲突。他们的来信刊登在《自然》第658卷，第572页。这一话题与更广泛的讨论相关，包括学术抵制、研究伙伴关系的变化，以及地缘政治紧张如何促使中国重新思考合作。", "传递的信息简单却紧迫：如果机构归属被当作政治身份，研究人员可能会因为与科学关系不大的原因，被切断与合作伙伴、数据和资金的联系。作者建议，更公平的规则将有助于在各国意见不合时仍保护合作。"], "body": "Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended result is appearing: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. In other words, the university or institute a researcher belongs to may now be read as a sign of their political side.\n\nThis concern was raised in a correspondence published in Nature on 06 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University in China and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They argue that fairer rules are needed to maintain global science collaboration during conflict.\n\nThe authors declare no competing interests. Their letter appears in volume 658 of Nature, page 572. The topic connects to wider debates, including academic boycotts, shifting research partnerships, and how geopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink collaboration. Several related articles in Nature also discuss ways to push back against politics stalling science.\n\nThe message is simple but urgent: if affiliation is treated as political identity, researchers may be cut off from partners, data, and funding for reasons that have little to do with science. Fairer rules, the authors suggest, would help protect collaboration even when countries disagree.", "body_zh": "长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益影响国际研究，一个意想不到的结果正在出现：机构归属正在变成政治身份的替身。换句话说，研究人员所属的大学或研究所，如今可能被解读为其政治立场的标志。\n\n这一担忧来自2026年10月6日发表在《自然》杂志上的一封通讯。作者是中国上海大学的Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh和位于金华的中国浙江师范大学的Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。他们认为，要在冲突期间维持全球科学合作，就需要更公平的规则。\n\n作者声明没有利益冲突。他们的来信刊登在《自然》第658卷，第572页。这一话题与更广泛的讨论相关，包括学术抵制、研究伙伴关系的变化，以及地缘政治紧张如何促使中国重新思考合作。\n\n传递的信息简单却紧迫：如果机构归属被当作政治身份，研究人员可能会因为与科学关系不大的原因，被切断与合作伙伴、数据和资金的联系。作者建议，更公平的规则将有助于在各国意见不合时仍保护合作。", "text": "Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended result is appearing: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. In other words, the university or institute a researcher belongs to may now be read as a sign of their political side. This concern was raised in a correspondence published in Nature on 06 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh of Shanghai University in China and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi of Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They argue that fairer rules are needed to maintain global science collaboration during conflict. The authors declare no competing interests. Their letter appears in volume 658 of Nature, page 572. The topic connects to wider debates, including academic boycotts, shifting research partnerships, and how geopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink collaboration. Several related articles in Nature also discuss ways to push back against politics stalling science. The message is simple but urgent: if affiliation is treated as political identity, researchers may be cut off from partners, data, and funding for reasons that have little to do with science. Fairer rules, the authors suggest, would help protect collaboration even when countries disagree.", "vocab": [{"word": "collaboration", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作", "note": "常与 global, scientific 搭配"}, {"word": "geopolitics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "地缘政治", "note": "指国家间政治与地理因素的关系"}, {"word": "unintended", "pos": "adj", "zh": "意想不到的", "note": "常修饰 consequence, result"}, {"word": "institutional affiliation", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "机构归属", "note": "指某人所属的大学或研究机构"}, {"word": "proxy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "替身；代理", "note": "a proxy for 表示“某物的替代指标”"}, {"word": "correspondence", "pos": "noun", "zh": "通讯；来信", "note": "在学术期刊中指读者来信"}, {"word": "declare", "pos": "verb", "zh": "声明", "note": "declare interests 声明利益关系"}, {"word": "competing interests", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "利益冲突", "note": "学术论文常用表达"}, {"word": "academic boycott", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "学术抵制", "note": "指拒绝与某些机构或国家合作"}, {"word": "partnership", "pos": "noun", "zh": "伙伴关系", "note": "research partnership 研究伙伴关系"}, {"word": "stall", "pos": "verb", "zh": "阻碍；停滞", "note": "可指使某事无法推进"}, {"word": "urgent", "pos": "adj", "zh": "紧迫的", "note": "常修饰 message, need, action"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collab